// A numeric key is read, not refused -- and the two spellings must agree.
//
// The `json` variant quotes a numeric key it can round-trip, so refusing them would refuse the first
// pass and then read `"42"` as an ordinary string key on the second, and formatting would not be
// idempotent. The key text therefore comes from the printer's own normaliser, which is why
// `/bare-numeric` and `/quoted-numeric` below come out in the same relative order.
//
// Every key here round-trips (`String(Number(k)) == k`), so its printed text is the property it names.
// The keys that do not are a different case entirely -- see `bail-aliased-numeric-key.json`.
//
// `/normaliser-matters` is the one that makes the normaliser observable, and it is why the other two
// are not enough: `1.50` and `"1.5"` are one property written twice, and they only tie -- and so only
// keep last-one-wins -- because the key is read as `1.5` rather than as its raw source text. Ordering
// by the raw slice reverses them and changes what the document means, with every other case here still
// passing.
//
// Note the ordering is by text, never numeric: `10` precedes `2`. The reference would answer `2, 10`.
{
  "openapi": "3.0.0",
  "paths": {
    "/bare-numeric": {
      "get": { "responses": {}, 0.5: "x", "operationId": "op", 10: "ten", 2: "two" }
    },
    "/quoted-numeric": {
      "get": { "responses": {}, "0.5": "x", "operationId": "op", "10": "ten", "2": "two" }
    },
    "/normaliser-matters": {
      "get": { "responses": {}, 1.50: "first", "1.5": "last", "operationId": "op" }
    }
  }
}
